From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenMyGlass <openmyglass@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 00:33:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: interactive: clamp targets above hispeed_freq
 instead of dropping them

Glass's kernel has a change in the speed-change task that AOSP does not
have: commit 82ecdfc2408b ("Interactive Governor: Add checks to prevent
deadlock in worker", Kai Yick, Google, November 2013) made the loop over
the policy's CPUs keep only targets at or below hispeed_freq. Its message
does not say why. The effects:

- hispeed_freq is the highest speed the governor ever sets, whatever
  above_hispeed_delay and target_loads ask for;
- a CPU whose target is above hispeed_freq is ignored, so the policy
  runs at the other CPU's lower target;
- when every CPU's target is above hispeed_freq, max_freq stays 0 and
  __cpufreq_driver_target(policy, 0, CPUFREQ_RELATION_H) selects the
  lowest table frequency within the policy (300 MHz by default),
  exactly when load is highest. The timer then sees the target it
  already chose and does not queue another change, so the speed can
  stay there until the target changes.

With the default hispeed_freq (the policy maximum, 1008 MHz on Glass)
no target can be above it, so stock behaviour is not affected. Any
lower hispeed_freq hits the last two cases.

Keep the ceiling, which may be intended (for example for heat), but
count a target above hispeed_freq as hispeed_freq. Every frequency set
stays at or below hispeed_freq, as before, and the policy no longer
drops to its lowest speed under load. hispeed_freq is read once per
policy so that every CPU is compared with the same value.

This change is licensed under GPL-2.0-only, like the file it changes.
---
 Documentation/cpu-freq/governors.txt  |  2 ++
 drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_interactive.c | 17 ++++++++++++++---
 2 files changed, 16 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Documentation/cpu-freq/governors.txt b/Documentation/cpu-freq/governors.txt
index 3e3e8e9..60d0ade 100644
--- a/Documentation/cpu-freq/governors.txt
+++ b/Documentation/cpu-freq/governors.txt
@@ -228,6 +228,8 @@ when CPU load hits the value specified in go_hispeed_load.  If load
 stays high for the amount of time specified in above_hispeed_delay,
 then speed may be bumped higher.  Default is the maximum speed
 allowed by the policy at governor initialization time.
+In this kernel, hispeed_freq is also the highest speed the governor
+sets: a CPU whose target is above it runs at hispeed_freq.
 
 go_hispeed_load: The CPU load at which to ramp to hispeed_freq.
 Default is 99%.
diff --git a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_interactive.c b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_interactive.c
index a1f0d08..7f08489 100644
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_interactive.c
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/cpufreq_interactive.c
@@ -561,6 +561,7 @@ static int cpufreq_interactive_speedchange_task(void *data)
 		for_each_cpu(cpu, &tmp_mask) {
 			unsigned int j;
 			unsigned int max_freq = 0;
+			unsigned int ceiling;
 
 			pcpu = &per_cpu(cpuinfo, cpu);
 			if (!down_read_trylock(&pcpu->enable_sem))
@@ -570,13 +571,23 @@ static int cpufreq_interactive_speedchange_task(void *data)
 				continue;
 			}
 
+			/*
+			 * hispeed_freq is the highest speed set here. A target
+			 * above it counts as hispeed_freq, instead of being
+			 * left out: when every CPU's target was above it,
+			 * max_freq stayed 0 and the policy dropped to its
+			 * lowest speed.
+			 */
+			ceiling = hispeed_freq;
+
 			for_each_cpu(j, pcpu->policy->cpus) {
 				struct cpufreq_interactive_cpuinfo *pjcpu =
 					&per_cpu(cpuinfo, j);
+				unsigned int freq = min(pjcpu->target_freq,
+							ceiling);
 
-				if ((pjcpu->target_freq > max_freq) &&
-					(pjcpu->target_freq <= hispeed_freq))
-					max_freq = pjcpu->target_freq;
+				if (freq > max_freq)
+					max_freq = freq;
 			}
 
 			if (max_freq != pcpu->policy->cur)
